{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: five property notes at exact 21ce7204 (branch planner/security-properties-notes-20261123, base main 12cb8fa7). Artifacts ad0b9839, 9c25b233, 942cfcd8, a4713f01, f781a054.\n\nI read all five notes and checked the points of request 9b4d7843 in source at the head. Three findings block. Five are minor.\n\nPoints confirmed\n- Note 2: `judgeActBody` is at packages/derive/src/judge.ts:239 and is already exported. packages/client calls it. `story.ts` imports `judgeAct`, `entryOf`, `applyEntry`, `judgeTimed` and `nextDue` (story.ts:5). The search adds no derive export. Ruling 12dff549 item 2 and docs/kernel.md section 7 name exploration as a caller of the one body judgment, so this caller is covered.\n- Note 3: `detail: guards.${guards.at}` is at packages/derive/src/program/body.ts:62. It indexes the act's own guard nodes. `publish` in examples/lending/definition.ts has one send and no guard. The five rules plus `notIn` and the two extent rows are the eight rows of the fail-open list in docs/security/definition-authors.md. Mechanism two removes no guard and runs one search, so it stays within the rule of request ecbc722a.\n- Note 4, section 1: every row matches definition.ts and roles.ts. `release` guards `on.signer(\"holder\")`. `return` guards only `on.in(\"lent\")`.\n- Note 5, section 3: `ApplicationPackage2` has `semantic` and `presentation` (application-v2-types.ts:30-31). I did not settle whether version 2 refuses an unknown member either. The note says so.\n- Ruling d6aa9122: each note states one guarantee. Note 1 leaves the shared-search question to Hugh.\n\nBlocking\n\nB1. Note 1, section 4: the probe route cannot judge a property on an item in a final state. judge.ts:414-416 refuses a `transition` on a final item with `final` before any guard runs. A `comment` may act on a final item, but validate/handlers.ts:263 gives a comment no guards. So `shelf-has-no-borrower`, which names `gone`, gets `final` for every gone thing and never a guard answer. This is the answer to the request's question: the route works without a kernel export for live states only. Fix by one of: (a) state properties name live states only in version 1, and the note drops `gone` from the Lending example; (b) the search checks a final-state property at the entry that enters the state, as a history property; (c) a guard-evaluation export from packages/derive, under kernel section 7. Name the choice and keep step 2's witness consistent with it. Also say that the copy with the probe act must pass `validateDefinition` to become a `ValidDefinition`, so a definition near the 64-act or 24-guard bound cannot take a probe.\n\nB2. Notes 3 and 5: the example `intended` answer misstates the act. \"The page text comes from the page\" is false. `publish` sends `f.content`, a 4 096-byte text field that the signer supplies. `borrow`, `return` and `cancel` send signer-supplied `content` to the page in the same way. So \"any member can republish a shelf page\" holds through four acts, not through the one unguarded act, and the no-guard finding is not the whole page-write story. Note 5, section 1 shows this answer to an agent as the release's word. Correct the sentence, for example \"Any member can write a shelf page's text; borrow, return and cancel also write it.\" Note 5 also shows that an `intended` answer is publisher text that no check compares with the forms. Say that in note 5, section 5.\n\nB3. Note 5, section 7: the kernel placement is incomplete. docs/kernel.md, table at line 49, says packages/platform fails the kernel test, because replay needs its code to derive platform scopes (K1). The install act is a rules-scope act with platform rules. If the install path decodes the package and version 2 refuses an unknown member, the note's own fallback, package version 3, changes what platform code accepts at installation. That is a K1 change and needs the section 7 review. State this condition beside the unread codec point. Then the build's first step reads the codec and settles whether section 7 applies.\n\nMinor\n\nM1. Note 3, section 2: `compile` already returns `findings` (packages/define/src/index.ts:641 and :707), with one rule today. The five rules extend that list. Say so rather than \"returns the findings beside the per-act refusals\".\nM2. Note 2, section 5: the phrase \"the runtime, replay and exploration\" is from docs/kernel.md section 7, not the text of ruling 12dff549. Cite the section, or quote the ruling's item 2.\nM3. Note 4: the guarantee covers each state and each slot, but the Lending table shows states only. Add the `borrower` rows (set by `borrow`, cleared by `return` and `cancel`), because note 1's two properties are about that slot.\nM4. Note 4, section 1: \"Signer must be: anyone\" for `reserve` hides its signer-reading `count` guard (at most one other held reservation by the signer). Section 2 reads only `signer` and `notIn`. Say that other signer-reading guards print in the row, as a rule does.\nM5. Note 1, section 6 and note 5, section 1: \"another member cannot take its held day\" is a claim in the model. Lending enforces it in `reserve`'s first guard for every history. Note 4's exact form could state it, and the note could say which form gives the stronger claim.\n\nConclusions\n\nArchitecture: sound. One search over the scope's own judges and fold has no second text to diverge. The authority table reads forms and claims every history. Properties stay out of the semantic digest. Note 1's evaluation route needs B1's choice before it is a design.\n\nSecurity: the notes add no authority path, and a scope never reads a property. They correctly bound what a pass claims: the model, the package roles against the room's live `set-actions`, platform rules, and the host. The risks are in the reader's text. B2 shows a publisher answer that misstates an act, and the agent in note 5 reads that answer. B3 is a kernel-boundary risk.\n\nSimplification: note 3, mechanism one, and note 4, step 1, need no search and stand alone. Start with them. Notes 1, 2 and 5 depend on the measurement of note 2, step 1. The 16-pair test is smaller until a second definition uses the search, as note 1 says. M1 removes a new mechanism in favour of the existing `findings`. Nothing else should go.\n\nChecks named in the request were not rerun. This review reads the text and the source.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ad0b9839149e017daa982b8899bb135ad5db3ccc","binding":"assigned","head":"21ce7204ee04b3f91851ae917832fba4fcc90f94","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16ff407b2f5112e9cd1e976bf285334d26b1128f\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:df3b7e9e5fa4f1782f6bafdd1c50dc8fee767745\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16ff407b2f5112e9cd1e976bf285334d26b1128f","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}